, "overview": "Glenn Grothman seeks another term in a northeastern Wisconsin district that leans Republican by about eight points. He enters the fall with a large financial advantage, roughly $929,000 raised and $830,000 on hand, while Democratic nominee Brad Smith has raised about $166,000. An independent, Michael Thurow, has also raised about $170,000, an unusual total for a third-party bid but still modest.\n\nEvery major rating outfit places the seat on the Republican side of the board, from Likely R to Safe R, and prediction markets give Smith roughly a six percent chance. The only polls available are dated and Democratic-sponsored, showing Grothman ahead by wide margins, including 61-39 in a head-to-head test. Smith has earned the state AFL-CIO endorsement, but the combined evidence points to a comfortable Grothman win, with the main question being the size of the margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0556, "margin": -10.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0454, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0701, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.063, "margin": -12.0}]}